  "datasheet": "https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-c6_datasheet_en.pdf",
  "verified": true,
  "summary": "Espressif entry-level RISC-V ESP32-C6 board (v1.2) with an ESP32-C6-WROOM-1(U) module (8 MB flash), Wi-Fi 6, BLE, Zigbee/Thread, two USB-C ports and an RGB LED on GPIO8.",
  "warnings": [
    {
      "level": "danger",
      "text": "3.3 V logic only. The GPIOs are not 5 V tolerant; never connect a 5 V signal to them."
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "Strapping pins GPIO4 (MTMS), GPIO5 (MTDI), GPIO8, GPIO9 and GPIO15 are read at reset. GPIO9 low = download mode; GPIO8 must be high for download mode and also drives the RGB LED. External pull-ups/pull-downs on them can stop the board from booting.",
      "pins": [
        "J1-3",
        "J1-4",
        "J1-9",
        "J3-11",
        "J3-4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "GPIO12/GPIO13 are the native USB D-/D+ of the ESP32-C6 USB-C port. GPIO16/GPIO17 are UART0, wired to the USB-to-UART bridge.",
      "pins": [
        "J3-14",
        "J3-13",
        "J3-2",
        "J3-3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "The ADC (ADC1 only) is on GPIO0-6. LP GPIOs (usable in deep sleep by the LP core) are GPIO0-7.",
      "pins": [
        "J1-7",
        "J1-8",
        "J1-12",
        "J1-13",
        "J1-3",
        "J1-4",
        "J1-5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "GPIO4-7 are the pin JTAG interface (MTMS, MTDI, MTCK, MTDO). They are free if you debug over USB Serial/JTAG.",
      "pins": [
        "J1-3",
        "J1-4",
        "J1-5",
        "J1-6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "J5 jumper (fitted by default) is for measuring module current. Removing it cuts the power between the module and the board peripherals (user guide)."
    }
  ],
